High-tension moments already in the code:
• The fuse turns clay in the last five seconds (Defuse).
• A burnt-out fuse relights shorter for another try (Defuse in the Game).
• The streak unlocks bass at 5, drums at 10 and the full track at 15; a broken streak is heard as well as seen (Mill, Pulse).
• The Trail ends the whole deal on a single miss.
• Rounds of six share three hearts, so the last heart ends the round (Use of English Part 4).
• The Word Lab's Next button fills like a fuse and fires on its own.
The gap is not invention but plumbing. The motion, beat, boss and path devices exist, but each is locked inside one page. Lifting them into the Game kit, the way Defuse and the pendulum already were, would let every topic's Game use them.
What the Writing games do differently
The Writing games are the only ones that work on a whole text rather than a single sentence. That is why they feel unlike the Challenge: the unit is a paragraph or an essay, and the student rebuilds it.
Game
The task
Why it is not exam-like
Sequence
Put an essay's paragraphs, then its sentences, back in order; labels appear only once earned
Structure at the size an examiner reads it
Storyboard
Read the text; it vanishes; bring back its linking frames and longer content words, while short words stay printed
Memory for structure and vocabulary, not a typing test
Puzzle
Rebuild each sentence word by word, with the punctuation kept on its word
Word order as the skill, sentence after sentence of one text
Builder
Build a paragraph from moves, picked from an action sheet
The student makes something rather than finds an answer
Three design choices are worth carrying over to the Game:
• One text, many moves. A single source carries a whole run, so the student's knowledge of the text grows as they play, instead of resetting on every cell.
• Vanish and recall. It is already proven across two skills: Speaking borrowed Storyboard as Recall, with a model answer whose phrases come back from memory.
• The card pauses. Closing the card stops both clocks, and reopening resumes exactly. That makes a long game safe to play on a phone in a lesson.
The look is its own as well: paper cut straight (no rounded corners), a one-pixel shadow, hairlines inside the card, a mono kicker and one Anton headline.
Gaps: raw material for the next two months
No game in the repository lets one answer change the next, or plays across the whole screen rather than inside a card. Those two gaps, plus the ones below, are where a Game could stop resembling the Challenge. Each gap names the material that already exists for it.
